# `sde::WienerPath`

A sampled path with its time grid: the data that the stochastic integrals integrate against.

A `WienerPath` is a plain value with four fields you can also fill yourself, for example from market data or from another simulator: the samples `w` (the time axis first), the grid `times`, the end points and the step count. Any path whose grid is strictly increasing and whose samples are finite can be integrated against; if it is not a standard Wiener path the integral calls still run and report, in their warnings, that the Itô and Stratonovich values are not the standard ones.

## Fields

### `w`

`pluss::Series`, default `undefined`.

Samples, shape `[steps + 1] + like.shape`; `w[0] = 0` for a simulated path.

### `times`

`std::vector<double>`, default `{}`.

The `steps + 1` grid times; finite and strictly increasing.

### `t0`

`double`, default `0.0`.

Start time.

### `t1`

`double`, default `0.0`.

End time; the integral calls compare the quadratic variation with `t1 − t0`.

### `steps`

`std::int64_t`, default `0`.

Number of steps; the samples and the grid must each have `steps + 1` entries.

## Guidance

Returned by `simulate_wiener_path` and accepted by the integral functions.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

The fields of a simulated path.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace sde = pluss::subsp::math::sde;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(3));
  const Series like = pluss::make_series(0.0).to(pluss::ScalarType::Float64).reshape({1});

  const sde::WienerPath path = sde::simulate_wiener_path(like, 0.5, 1.5, 4, 2);
  out.writeln("t0 =", path.t0, ", t1 =", path.t1, ", steps =", path.steps);
  out.writeln("times =", path.times);
  out.writeln("w shape =", path.w.sizes());
}
```

### Example 2

A hand-made path. Any `Series` of samples with a matching grid is a path. A straight line from 0 to 1 is smooth, with a quadratic variation of `1/steps`, far below the elapsed time, and the result says so; for a smooth path the Itô and Stratonovich integrals coincide at `½`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace sde = pluss::subsp::math::sde;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const std::int64_t n = 400;
  sde::WienerPath path;
  path.w = (pluss::series::arange(0.0, static_cast<double>(n + 1), 1.0, pluss::ScalarType::Float64) / static_cast<double>(n)).reshape({n + 1, 1});
  for (std::int64_t i = 0; i <= n; ++i) path.times.push_back(static_cast<double>(i) / static_cast<double>(n));
  path.t0 = 0.0;
  path.t1 = 1.0;
  path.steps = n;
  const auto r = sde::ito_integral([](double, const Series &w) { return w; }, path);
  out.writeln("integral of W dW =", r.value.items<double>(), ", quadratic variation ratio =", r.quadratic_variation_ratio);
  out.writeln("warnings =", r.warnings.size());
}
```

### Example 3

An inconsistent path is refused. Here the grid has the wrong length for the samples.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace sde = pluss::subsp::math::sde;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  sde::WienerPath path;
  path.w = pluss::make_series({0.0, 0.5, 1.0}, {3, 1});
  path.times = {0.0, 1.0};
  path.t0 = 0.0;
  path.t1 = 1.0;
  path.steps = 2;
  try {
    (void)sde::ito_integral([](double, const Series &w) { return w; }, path);
  } catch (const std::exception &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    out.writeln(text.substr(0, text.find('\n')));
  }
}
```

